Do not substitute only one value of $x$. One substitution gives only one equation. It cannot solve for several unknowns.

Polynomial long division repeats four steps until the remainder has a lower degree than the divisor:
(1) divide the leading terms to get the next term of the quotient;
(2) multiply that term by the whole divisor and line it up underneath;
(3) subtract (change every sign, then add);
(4) bring down the next term and repeat.
For $2x^{3}-5x^{2}-4x+7$ divided by $2x-5$, the working is written like this:
{{math:0}}
Round 1: $2x^{3}\div 2x=x^{2}$ and $x^{2}(2x-5)=2x^{3}-5x^{2}$; subtracting cancels both the $x^{3}$ and $x^{2}$ terms, so bring down $-4x$.
Round 2: $-4x\div 2x=-2$ and $-2(2x-5)=-4x+10$; subtracting leaves $-3$, a constant (degree 0). It is lower than the divisor's degree 1, so stop.
The division algorithm links the quotient $Q(x)$ and the remainder $R(x)$:
{{math:1}}
Two rules to remember: the degree of the remainder must be lower than the degree of the divisor; the degree of the dividend equals the degree of the divisor plus the degree of the quotient.
{{math:2}}
If a term is missing, add $0x$ (write $x^{3}+8x^{2}-8$ as $x^{3}+8x^{2}+0x-8$) so the columns stay aligned.
