A rational function is one polynomial divided by another. Adding and subtracting works exactly like fractions: get a common denominator first.
(1) Factorise every denominator.
(2) Take the L.C.M. of the denominators as the common denominator.
(3) Put the whole numerator in brackets; after a minus sign every term changes sign.
(4) Collect like terms, then cancel common factors.
{{math:0}}
The commonest slip in subtraction is applying the minus sign to the first term only:
{{math:1}}
{{math:2}}
So the second numerator must be written as $-5(x+4)=-5x-20$, not $-5x+4$.
If the denominators are $(x-5)$ and $(5-x)$, they differ only by a sign: $-(x-5)=5-x$. Multiply the numerator and denominator by $-1$:
{{math:3}}
After getting a common denominator, check the numerator: only complete common factors may be cancelled, never a single $x$ or a single term.
